@flexiarg argument-coherence/say-how-it-carries-back
@title Say How the Result Carries Back to the Audience
@keywords benefit, audience, impact, return, who benefits
@audience paper and proposal authors, reviewers, who-benefits analysts
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Mined from the Peeragogy "Greatest Flops" archive (~/notes/peeragogy-flops): the Wikimedia IEG 2013 comments on flop 5 (appendices/05, 'Comments'). Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to test blind on the other flops, with Corneli 2016 (ICCC) and the Peeragogy Handbook as controls that should pass
@references [argument-coherence/ARGUMENT argument-coherence/evidence-the-venue-counts argument-coherence/earn-the-coined-term argument-coherence/one-worked-instance argument-coherence/show-the-deliverable argument-coherence/plan-at-reviewable-grain argument-coherence/claims-as-hypotheses]

! conclusion:
  A proposal says concretely how its result returns to the audience being asked to support it: who uses the result, in which of their activities, and how they will notice.

  + context:
    Proposals describe the work and its general value. The funder or community asks what it gets back.

  + IF:
    An argument asks an audience for money, time, or attention.

  + HOWEVER:
    Promising specific uptake can overclaim; some value is real but diffuse.

  + THEN:
    Name at least one concrete route from the result to the audience's own work, and say how that route would be checked.

  + BECAUSE:
    Flop 5's Wikimedia reviewers asked: 'Do you have a plan for how a handbook that incorporates learnings from Wikimedians' practices would have then carry back to have impact on Wikimedia communities?' and 'Can you briefly address some of the concrete ways that the resources/infrastructure (patterns, tools, etc) generated through this project will be used to support Wikimedia projects?'

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Test blind: a reviewer given only a flop and this family; compare its rows with the recorded verdicts.]
    next[Related: The who-benefits side: institutions/appropriation-and-provision and creativity-commons/benefit-proportional-to-contribution.]
